  "worked_solution": "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\u27e8x,z\u27e9=\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}\u2061t+{sin}^{2}\u2061t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t.$",
